```csharp
public class Exposure<T>
{
    public T Value1 { get; set; }
    public T Value2 { get; set; }
    public T Value3 { get; set; }

    public Exposure(T value1, T value2, T value3)
    {
        Value1 = value1;
        Value2 = value2;
        Value3 = value3;
    }

    public Exposure<U> Map<U>(Func<T,U> func)
    {
        U newValue1 = func(Value1); 
        U newValue2 = func(Value2);
        U newValue3 = func(Value3);

        return new Exposure<U>(newValue1, newValue2, newValue3);
    }

    public Exposure<U> Zip<U>(Exposure<U> other, Func<T,U,U> func)
    {
        U newValue1 = func(Value1, other.Value1);
        U newValue2 = func(Value2, other.Value2); 
        U newValue3 = func(Value3, other.Value3);

        return new Exposure<U>(newValue1, newValue2, newValue3);
    }
}
```

The `Map()` function applies the given function to all 3 values, returning a new `Exposure` with a potentially different generic type.

The `Zip()` function zips the values with those from another `Exposure` instance, applying the given function and returning a new `Exposure` result.

