CRTP stands for Curiously Recurring Template Pattern. It is a technique in C++ that allows you to specialize a template class for a particular type at compile time. This can be useful for a variety of purposes, such as implementing virtual functions, implementing polymorphic behavior, and creating type-safe containers.

One example of CRTP is the implementation of the `std::unique_ptr` class. `std::unique_ptr` is a smart pointer that owns the object it points to and automatically deletes it when it goes out of scope. The `unique_ptr` class is implemented using CRTP as follows:

```c++
template <typename T>
class unique_ptr {
public:
  unique_ptr(T* ptr = nullptr) : ptr(ptr) {}
  ~unique_ptr() { delete ptr; }

  T* get() const { return ptr; }
  void reset(T* ptr = nullptr) { delete ptr; ptr = ptr; }

private:
  T* ptr;
};
```

