In the `OnEnable` method, we enable the mouse input device and add a listener for device changes to enable any newly connected mouse devices. In the `OnDisable` method, we disable the mouse input device.

To run code when the user refreshes the browser in React, you can use the `useEffect` hook. The `useEffect` hook is executed after every render, which means that it will be executed every time the user refreshes the browser.

To use the `useEffect` hook, you pass it a function as an argument. The function will be executed after every render.

For example, the following code will run the function `myFunction` after every render:

```
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

useEffect(() => {
  myFunction();
}, []);
```

The `[]` at the end of the argument list is called the dependencies array. The dependencies array is a list of variables that the function depends on. If any of the variables in the dependencies array change, the function will be executed again.

In the above example, the `count` state variable is the only dependency. So, if the `count` state variable changes, the `myFunction` function will be executed again.

You can also use the `useEffect` hook to run code when the user navigates to a different page. To do this, you pass the `{ pathname }` object as the first argument to the `useEffect` hook.

For example, the following code will run the function `myFunction` when the user navigates to the `/about` page:

```
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

useEffect(() => {
  if (location.pathname === '/about') {
    myFunction();
  }
}, [location.pathname]);
```

The `useEffect` hook is a powerful tool that can be used to run code in response to changes in the state of your application or the URL of the current page.

